#
# csum16: p4c's six vectors, from testdata/p4_16_samples/issue655-bmv2.stf,
# verbatim. Only these comments were added. The expected bytes were produced
# by BMv2, so they pin p4blo's checksum16 to what a real target computes,
# including at the 0xFFFF/0x0000 edge of one's-complement arithmetic.
#
# Each packet is one H: d then c, 16 bits each. The control increments d and
# then sets c = checksum16.compute(d), the one's complement of the
# one's-complement sum of the 16-bit words of {d}. Over a single word the
# sum is d itself, so c = ~d, and the two fields of every output sum to
# 0xFFFF. The input c is ignored: verify_checksum is deferred, and
# update_checksum overwrites it.
#
#   d in    d + 1   c = ~(d + 1)
#   0x0000  0x0001  0xFFFE
#   0x0001  0x0002  0xFFFD
#   0xFFFF  0x0000  0xFFFF        d wraps to zero; the checksum of zero is
#                                 all ones, not zero
#   0xFFFE  0xFFFF  0x0000        a word of all ones sums to 0xFFFF, whose
#                                 complement is 0x0000
#   0xFFFD  0xFFFE  0x0001
#   0xFFFE  0xFFFF  0x0000        as above, with a different input c
#
# The program declares no egress_port, so every packet leaves on port 0.

packet 0 0000 0000
expect 0 0001 FFFE
packet 0 0001 FFFE
expect 0 0002 FFFD
packet 0 FFFF 0000
expect 0 0000 FFFF
packet 0 FFFE 1000
expect 0 FFFF 0000
packet 0 FFFD 1000
expect 0 FFFE 0001
packet 0 FFFE 0001
expect 0 FFFF 0000
